// EXPECT: [E011]
// ★ THE ROW IS COMPLETE, which is what makes it worth reading. Widening the bound by one effect
// does not grant the others — otherwise `effects io` would mean "stop checking effects here", and
// a caller could not trust a signature at all. The default is ∅ for everything, so every deviation
// appears in the row or not at all.
//
// This declares `io` and also diverges, and the CODE is the interesting part:
//
//   · it used to be [E130], from the declared-vs-inferred comparison ("it also has: diverge");
//   · it is now [E011] from the TERMINATION pass, which names the loop and the three ways out.
//
// `diverge` is the one effect with a sovereign verdict of its own, and the termination seam stands
// it down in BOTH effect checks so that verdict is the one heard. A row complaint at the function
// is strictly less useful than a diagnostic at the loop header. The companion tests pin the scope
// of that standing-down from both sides: seam_diverge_loop_still_refused_fail.ln (the loop is still
// refused) and seam_opaque_diverge_kept_fail.ln (divergence BELIEVED from an extern's row is never
// stood down, and reports as [E130] because nothing else would mention it).
//
// The row-covers-body path itself is still tested, by effects_alloc_bound_fail.ln — `alloc` has no
// verdict of its own, so there the comparison is what speaks.
extern func libc_printf(fmt *u8, ...) i32 effects io
func serve(n i32) i32 effects io {
    var i i32 = 0
    while i < n { libc_printf("x") }
    return i
}
func main() i32 { return 0 }
